 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "An embedded system is a small computer built into a larger device to do one special job. It has a microcontroller (processor, memory, input and output on one chip), sensors (inputs) and actuators (outputs such as motors, lights, buzzers).\nExamples: washing machines, microwave ovens, digital watches, traffic lights, car engine controls, ATMs, printers, air conditioners.\nThe Internet of Things (IoT) is a network of everyday objects with embedded systems that connect to the internet and share data: smart TVs, smart meters (prepaid electricity), smart watches, home security cameras, smart farms, smart city lights.\nHow IoT works: a sensor collects data → the microcontroller processes it → the data is sent over the internet → an app or the cloud uses it → an actuator acts.\nAdvantages: automation, remote control, saving energy and water. Disadvantages: security and privacy risks, needs internet and power, cost.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "x": "a smart farm waters the crops when the soil is dry"
  },
  "problem": "Describe how {x}.",
  "steps": [
   "A soil moisture sensor measures the water in the soil.",
   "The microcontroller decides the soil is too dry and switches on the pump (actuator); the farmer can see the data on a phone."
  ],
  "answer": "Sensor → microcontroller → pump, with the data sent to the farmer's phone.",
  "checks": []
 },
